 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Non-Power-of-Two Dimensions:** The Z-order layout is optimized for dimensions that are even powers of two. If dimensions are not powers of two, padding is applied, inflating the memory footprint by 1.5× to 4× depending on the deviation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Allocation:** The conversion step `zoConvertArrayOrderToZOrder` always allocates a new array. There is no zero-copy in-place conversion. Data must be loaded in Z-order format initially if memory allocation is a constraint [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Non-Power-of-Two Dimensions:** The layout is optimized for dimensions that are even powers of two. If dimensions are not powers of two, padding is applied, inflating the memory footprint by 1.5× to 4× depending on the deviation [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Allocation:** The conversion step always allocates a new array. There is no zero-copy in-place conversion. Data must be loaded in Z-order format initially if memory allocation is a constraint [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Preprocessing Cost:** Many datasets are not stored in Z-order initially, incurring a re-ordering cost that is amortized if multiple visualizations are generated from the same dataset [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Neighbor Proximity:** High-dimensional neighbors are not always close when mapped linearly, which can affect the efficiency of operations relying on true spatial proximity [S4].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Morton-Order Curve
-The mathematical basis for Z-order, involving bit-interleaving. It is the standard implementation of Z-order in many graphics libraries. [Link to sibling if available, otherwise general reference].
+### Morton-order curve
+The standard bit-interleaving implementation of Z-order. It sets itself apart by providing the fastest index computation among space-filling curves. It is chosen when computational speed outweighs marginal locality gains.
 
-### Hilbert Curve
-An alternative space-filling curve that offers better locality than Z-order but is significantly more expensive to compute. Z-order is preferred in volume rendering for its speed [S2, unofficial].
+### Hilbert curve
+A space-filling curve that preserves spatial locality better than Z-order but requires significantly more complex arithmetic to compute indices. It is chosen when strict neighborhood preservation is critical and index computation cost is acceptable.
 
